{
 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Unit 2 Assignment 2\n",
    "## Matrix Algebra & Systems of Equations\n",
   ],
   "id": "cell-00"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<a id=\"Setup\"></a>\n",
    "# Setup"
   ],
   "id": "cell-01"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 1,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "\n",
    "np.set_printoptions(suppress=True)"
   ],
   "id": "cell-02"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "<a id=\"Mission-1\"></a>\n",
    "# Mission 1 — Understanding the Matrix\n",
    "\n",
    "## Tasks\n",
    "- Represent the dataset as a matrix\n",
    "- Determine the matrix shape (rows and columns)\n",
    "- Explain what each row and each column represents\n",
    "- Create the matrix using NumPy\n",
    "\n",
    "## Dataset\n",
    "\n",
    "| House | Area | Bedrooms | Age |\n",
    "|-------|------|----------|-----|\n",
    "| H1 | 120 | 3 | 10 |\n",
    "| H2 | 150 | 4 | 8 |\n",
    "| H3 | 180 | 4 | 6 |\n",
    "| H4 | 210 | 5 | 4 |\n",
    "\n",
    "## Matrix representation\n",
    "Only the numeric values are kept. The columns are ordered as **Area, Bedrooms, Age**.\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "X = \\begin{bmatrix} 120 & 3 & 10 \\\\ 150 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 180 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 210 & 5 & 4 \\end{bmatrix}\n",
    "$$"
   ],
   "id": "cell-03"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 2,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "output_type": "stream",
     "name": "stdout",
     "text": [
      "X =\n",
      "[[120   3  10]\n",
      " [150   4   8]\n",
      " [180   4   6]\n",
      " [210   5   4]]\n",
      "\n",
      "Shape  : (4, 3)\n",
      "Rows   : 4\n",
      "Columns: 3\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "X = np.array([[120, 3, 10],\n",
    "              [150, 4,  8],\n",
    "              [180, 4,  6],\n",
    "              [210, 5,  4]])\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"X =\")\n",
    "print(X)\n",
    "print()\n",
    "print(\"Shape  :\", X.shape)\n",
    "print(\"Rows   :\", X.shape[0])\n",
    "print(\"Columns:\", X.shape[1])"
   ],
   "id": "cell-04"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Explanation\n",
    "- The matrix has **4 rows** and **3 columns**, so its shape is **4 × 3**.\n",
    "- **Each row** represents one house (one sample).\n",
    "- **Each column** represents one feature measured for every house: Area, Bedrooms, or Age.\n",
    "\n",
    "## AI Insight: What do rows and columns represent in Machine Learning?\n",
    "Rows are **samples** (data points) and columns are **features**. The standard layout for ML input data is `(n_samples × n_features)`, which is exactly what `X` is here."
   ],
   "id": "cell-05"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "<a id=\"Mission-2\"></a>\n",
    "# Mission 2 — Matrix Operations\n",
    "\n",
    "## Tasks\n",
    "- Add 2 to every Age value\n",
    "- Subtract 1 from every Age value\n",
    "- Multiply the Area column by 1.1\n",
    "- Compute the transpose\n",
    "- Verify every operation using NumPy"
   ],
   "id": "cell-06"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 3,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "output_type": "stream",
     "name": "stdout",
     "text": [
      "Age + 2    : [12. 10.  8.  6.]\n",
      "Age - 1    : [9. 7. 5. 3.]\n",
      "Area x 1.1 : [132. 165. 198. 231.]\n",
      "\n",
      "X^T =\n",
      "[[120 150 180 210]\n",
      " [  3   4   4   5]\n",
      " [ 10   8   6   4]]\n",
      "Shape of X^T: (3, 4)\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "Xf = X.astype(float)   # float is needed because Area * 1.1 is not an integer\n",
    "\n",
    "# 1) Add 2 to every Age value\n",
    "X_age_plus2 = Xf.copy()\n",
    "X_age_plus2[:, 2] += 2\n",
    "\n",
    "# 2) Subtract 1 from every Age value\n",
    "X_age_minus1 = Xf.copy()\n",
    "X_age_minus1[:, 2] -= 1\n",
    "\n",
    "# 3) Multiply the Area column by 1.1\n",
    "X_area_scaled = Xf.copy()\n",
    "X_area_scaled[:, 0] *= 1.1\n",
    "\n",
    "# 4) Transpose\n",
    "XT = X.T\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Age + 2    :\", X_age_plus2[:, 2])\n",
    "print(\"Age - 1    :\", X_age_minus1[:, 2])\n",
    "print(\"Area x 1.1 :\", np.round(X_area_scaled[:, 0], 2))\n",
    "print()\n",
    "print(\"X^T =\")\n",
    "print(XT)\n",
    "print(\"Shape of X^T:\", XT.shape)"
   ],
   "id": "cell-07"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Verification\n",
    "Each result is checked a second, independent way (broadcasting with a vector instead of column indexing)."
   ],
   "id": "cell-08"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 4,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "output_type": "stream",
     "name": "stdout",
     "text": [
      "All Mission 2 operations verified\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "assert np.allclose(X_age_plus2,   Xf + np.array([0, 0,  2]))\n",
    "assert np.allclose(X_age_minus1,  Xf + np.array([0, 0, -1]))\n",
    "assert np.allclose(X_area_scaled, Xf * np.array([1.1, 1, 1]))\n",
    "\n",
    "assert XT.shape == (X.shape[1], X.shape[0])\n",
    "assert np.array_equal(XT.T, X)              # transposing twice returns the original\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"All Mission 2 operations verified\")"
   ],
   "id": "cell-09"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## AI Insight\n",
    "- **Which operation changes values?** Adding, subtracting, and multiplying by a number. The shape stays 4 × 3.\n",
    "- **Which operation changes structure?** Transpose. The shape changes from 4 × 3 to 3 × 4, while the values stay the same.\n",
    "- **Why is transpose useful?** It makes matrix dimensions compatible for multiplication, it appears in the linear regression formula $X^TX$ and in covariance calculations, and it converts between row vectors and column vectors."
   ],
   "id": "cell-10"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "<a id=\"Mission-3\"></a>\n",
    "# Mission 3 — AI Prediction\n",
    "\n",
    "## Tasks\n",
    "- Create the weight matrix\n",
    "- Perform matrix multiplication\n",
    "- Compute the prediction score\n",
    "- Verify using NumPy\n",
    "\n",
    "## Weights\n",
    "\n",
    "| Feature | Weight |\n",
    "|---------|--------|\n",
    "| Area | 2 |\n",
    "| Bedrooms | 20 |\n",
    "| Age | -5 |\n",
    "\n",
    "The weights form a column matrix of shape **3 × 1**. Multiplying the data matrix (4 × 3) by the weights (3 × 1) is valid because the inner dimensions match, and it gives one score per house: a **4 × 1** result.\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\hat{y} = X \\, w\n",
    "$$"
   ],
   "id": "cell-11"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 5,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "output_type": "stream",
     "name": "stdout",
     "text": [
      "Weight matrix w (shape (3, 1) ):\n",
      "[[ 2]\n",
      " [20]\n",
      " [-5]]\n",
      "\n",
      "Prediction scores (shape (4, 1) ):\n",
      "  H1: 250\n",
      "  H2: 340\n",
      "  H3: 410\n",
      "  H4: 500\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "w = np.array([[2],\n",
    "              [20],\n",
    "              [-5]])\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Weight matrix w (shape\", w.shape, \"):\")\n",
    "print(w)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Matrix multiplication\n",
    "scores = X @ w\n",
    "\n",
    "print()\n",
    "print(\"Prediction scores (shape\", scores.shape, \"):\")\n",
    "for i, s in enumerate(scores.flatten(), start=1):\n",
    "    print(f\"  H{i}: {s}\")"
   ],
   "id": "cell-12"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Verification"
   ],
   "id": "cell-13"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 6,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "output_type": "stream",
     "name": "stdout",
     "text": [
      "Prediction scores verified\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "assert np.array_equal(scores, np.dot(X, w))                          # np.dot\n",
    "assert np.array_equal(scores.flatten(), np.sum(X * w.T, axis=1))     # row-by-row weighted sum\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Prediction scores verified\")"
   ],
   "id": "cell-14"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## AI Insight: Where is matrix multiplication used in AI?\n",
    "- **Linear regression:** $\\hat{y} = Xw$\n",
    "- **Neural networks:** every layer computes $Wx + b$\n",
    "- **Transformers:** the attention mechanism relies on $QK^T$\n",
    "- **Recommendation systems and embeddings**\n",
    "- **Computer vision (CNNs)**\n",
    "\n",
    "GPUs are designed to run matrix multiplication very fast, which is why they power modern AI."
   ],
   "id": "cell-15"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "<a id=\"Mission-4\"></a>\n",
    "# Mission 4 — Detecting Redundant Features\n",
    "\n",
    "## Tasks\n",
    "- Create the new matrix\n",
    "- Compute the rank\n",
    "- Verify using NumPy\n",
    "- Explain your findings\n",
    "\n",
    "## New dataset (with the Rooms feature)\n",
    "\n",
    "| House | Area | Bedrooms | Age | Rooms |\n",
    "|-------|------|----------|-----|-------|\n",
    "| H1 | 120 | 3 | 10 | 6 |\n",
    "| H2 | 150 | 4 | 8 | 8 |\n",
    "| H3 | 180 | 4 | 6 | 8 |\n",
    "| H4 | 210 | 5 | 4 | 10 |\n",
    "\n",
    "Notice that **Rooms = Bedrooms × 2**."
   ],
   "id": "cell-16"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 7,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "output_type": "stream",
     "name": "stdout",
     "text": [
      "X2 shape: (4, 4)\n",
      "Rank of X2: 3\n",
      "Rooms == 2 * Bedrooms ? True\n",
      "Rank without the Rooms column: 3\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "X2 = np.array([[120, 3, 10,  6],\n",
    "               [150, 4,  8,  8],\n",
    "               [180, 4,  6,  8],\n",
    "               [210, 5,  4, 10]])\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"X2 shape:\", X2.shape)\n",
    "print(\"Rank of X2:\", np.linalg.matrix_rank(X2))\n",
    "print(\"Rooms == 2 * Bedrooms ?\", np.array_equal(X2[:, 3], 2 * X2[:, 1]))\n",
    "print(\"Rank without the Rooms column:\", np.linalg.matrix_rank(X2[:, :3]))"
   ],
   "id": "cell-17"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Findings\n",
    "- `X2` is a **4 × 4** matrix, but its rank is only **3**. It is **rank-deficient**.\n",
    "- The Rooms column is exactly 2 × the Bedrooms column, so it carries **no new information**.\n",
    "- Removing Rooms keeps the rank at 3, which confirms that the other three features (Area, Bedrooms, Age) are linearly independent.\n",
    "\n",
    "## AI Insight: Why are redundant features harmful?\n",
    "- They cause **multicollinearity**: $X^TX$ becomes singular (or nearly singular) and cannot be inverted reliably.\n",
    "- Model weights become **unstable** and hard to interpret.\n",
    "- They increase the risk of **overfitting** and add computational cost without adding information."
   ],
   "id": "cell-18"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "<a id=\"Mission-5\"></a>\n",
    "# Mission 5 — Determinant, Identity Matrix & Inverse\n",
    "\n",
    "## Tasks\n",
    "- Compute the determinant of A and B and verify with NumPy\n",
    "- Verify $A \\times I = A$ and $B \\times I = B$\n",
    "- Determine which matrix is invertible and compute its inverse\n",
    "- Explain why the other matrix has no inverse\n",
    "\n",
    "## Matrices\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "A = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}, \\qquad\n",
    "B = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}, \\qquad\n",
    "I = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}\n",
    "$$"
   ],
   "id": "cell-19"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 8,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "output_type": "stream",
     "name": "stdout",
     "text": [
      "det(A) = 0.0\n",
      "det(B) = 1.0\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "A = np.array([[2, 1],\n",
    "              [4, 2]])\n",
    "B = np.array([[2, 1],\n",
    "              [3, 2]])\n",
    "I = np.eye(2)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Determinants (rounded to remove floating-point noise)\n",
    "det_A = round(np.linalg.det(A), 10)\n",
    "det_B = round(np.linalg.det(B), 10)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"det(A) =\", det_A)\n",
    "print(\"det(B) =\", det_B)"
   ],
   "id": "cell-20"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Identity matrix check"
   ],
   "id": "cell-21"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 9,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "output_type": "stream",
     "name": "stdout",
     "text": [
      "A x I = A ? True\n",
      "B x I = B ? True\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "print(\"A x I = A ?\", np.allclose(A @ I, A))\n",
    "print(\"B x I = B ?\", np.allclose(B @ I, B))"
   ],
   "id": "cell-22"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Invertibility\n",
    "A matrix is invertible only if its determinant is non-zero."
   ],
   "id": "cell-23"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 10,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "output_type": "stream",
     "name": "stdout",
     "text": [
      "A invertible? False\n",
      "B invertible? True\n",
      "\n",
      "B^-1 =\n",
      "[[ 2. -1.]\n",
      " [-3.  2.]]\n",
      "\n",
      "B @ B^-1 =\n",
      "[[1. 0.]\n",
      " [0. 1.]]\n",
      "\n",
      "inv(A) raised LinAlgError: Singular matrix\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "print(\"A invertible?\", det_A != 0)\n",
    "print(\"B invertible?\", det_B != 0)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Inverse of B\n",
    "B_inv = np.linalg.inv(B)\n",
    "print()\n",
    "print(\"B^-1 =\")\n",
    "print(B_inv)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Check: B x B^-1 = I\n",
    "print()\n",
    "print(\"B @ B^-1 =\")\n",
    "print(np.round(B @ B_inv, 10))\n",
    "assert np.allclose(B @ B_inv, I)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Inverse of A fails\n",
    "print()\n",
    "try:\n",
    "    np.linalg.inv(A)\n",
    "except np.linalg.LinAlgError as e:\n",
    "    print(\"inv(A) raised LinAlgError:\", e)"
   ],
   "id": "cell-24"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Findings\n",
    "- **Matrix B is invertible** because det(B) = 1 ≠ 0. NumPy returns $B^{-1} = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$, and $B \\times B^{-1} = I$.\n",
    "- **Matrix A has no inverse** because det(A) = 0. The second row of A is exactly 2 × its first row, so the rows are linearly dependent, and the inverse formula would require dividing by zero.\n",
    "\n",
    "## AI Insight\n",
    "- **What does determinant = 0 mean?** The matrix is singular: its rows (or columns) are linearly dependent, and it collapses space into a lower dimension.\n",
    "- **Why can't a singular matrix be inverted?** Different inputs get mapped to the same output, so the original input cannot be recovered. The inverse formula also divides by the determinant.\n",
    "- **Why is the inverse important in Machine Learning?** The closed-form solution of linear regression, $w = (X^TX)^{-1}X^Ty$, needs an inverse. When $X^TX$ is singular (for example because of redundant features), we use the pseudo-inverse or Ridge regularization instead."
   ],
   "id": "cell-25"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "<a id=\"Mission-6\"></a>\n",
    "# Mission 6 — Solving Linear Systems\n",
    "\n",
    "## Tasks\n",
    "- Write the matrix representation\n",
    "- Solve using NumPy\n",
    "- Compare the solutions\n",
    "\n",
    "## System\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\begin{cases} 2x + y = 7 \\\\ x - y = 2 \\end{cases}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "## Matrix representation ($A\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{b}$)\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\end{bmatrix}\n",
    "\\begin{bmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{bmatrix} =\n",
    "\\begin{bmatrix} 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{bmatrix}\n",
    "$$"
   ],
   "id": "cell-26"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 11,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "output_type": "stream",
     "name": "stdout",
     "text": [
      "Method 1 (np.linalg.solve):  x = 3.0 , y = 1.0\n",
      "Method 2 (A^-1 @ b)       :  x = 3.0 , y = 1.0\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "A6 = np.array([[2,  1],\n",
    "               [1, -1]])\n",
    "b6 = np.array([7, 2])\n",
    "\n",
    "# Method 1: np.linalg.solve\n",
    "sol_solve = np.linalg.solve(A6, b6)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Method 2: inverse matrix, v = A^-1 b\n",
    "sol_inv = np.linalg.inv(A6) @ b6\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Method 1 (np.linalg.solve):  x =\", sol_solve[0], \", y =\", sol_solve[1])\n",
    "print(\"Method 2 (A^-1 @ b)       :  x =\", round(sol_inv[0], 10), \", y =\", round(sol_inv[1], 10))"
   ],
   "id": "cell-27"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Comparison and verification"
   ],
   "id": "cell-28"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 12,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "output_type": "stream",
     "name": "stdout",
     "text": [
      "Both methods agree? True\n",
      "A @ v = [7. 2.]  (expected [7 2] )\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "print(\"Both methods agree?\", np.allclose(sol_solve, sol_inv))\n",
    "\n",
    "# Substitute the solution back into the original equations\n",
    "print(\"A @ v =\", A6 @ sol_solve, \" (expected\", b6, \")\")\n",
    "assert np.allclose(A6 @ sol_solve, b6)"
   ],
   "id": "cell-29"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Findings\n",
    "Both methods give the same solution: **x = 3, y = 1**, and substituting it back reproduces the right-hand side `[7, 2]`.\n",
    "\n",
    "## AI Insight: Why are systems of equations important in Machine Learning?\n",
    "Training a linear regression model reduces to solving the system $(X^TX)w = X^Ty$ for the optimal weights. Least squares, many optimization algorithms, and the fitting of linear models all rely on solving linear systems."
   ],
   "id": "cell-30"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "<a id=\"Summary\"></a>\n",
    "# Summary\n",
    "\n",
    "| Mission | Result |\n",
    "|---------|--------|\n",
    "| 1 | Data matrix is 4 × 3: 4 samples (houses), 3 features |\n",
    "| 2 | Value operations keep the shape; transpose changes it to 3 × 4 |\n",
    "| 3 | Prediction scores: H1 = 250, H2 = 340, H3 = 410, H4 = 500 |\n",
    "| 4 | Rank = 3 < 4, so Rooms is a redundant feature |\n",
    "| 5 | det(A) = 0 (singular), det(B) = 1, B is invertible |\n",
    "| 6 | x = 3, y = 1 |"
   ],
   "id": "cell-31"
  }
 ],
